---
import SiteShell from '../layouts/SiteShell.astro';

const title = 'Legend of Mana Decompilation Project';
const description = 'A matching decompilation of the North American PlayStation release of Legend of Mana.';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---
<SiteShell {title} {description} active="home">
  <article class="home-page">
    <section class="content-panel intro-panel" aria-labelledby="what-title">
      <h2 id="what-title" class="section-ribbon ribbon-green">What is this?</h2>
      <div class="section-copy lead-copy">
        <p><strong>lom-decomp</strong> is a community project to reconstruct the source code of the North American PlayStation release of <em>Legend of Mana</em>.</p>
        <p>The original game was released as compiled machine code, not as the human-readable source code its developers wrote. We study that compiled program and rebuild its logic as clean C source code.</p>
        <p>The aim is not to rewrite the game from memory or make something that merely behaves similarly. We want the reconstructed code to reproduce the original program as precisely as possible.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="content-panel" aria-labelledby="decomp-title">
      <h2 id="decomp-title" class="section-ribbon ribbon-blue">What is decompilation?</h2>
      <div class="section-copy illustrated-copy">
        <div>
          <p>A computer does not run C source code directly. The source is compiled into machine instructions that the PlayStation's processor can execute.</p>
          <p><strong>Decompilation works in the opposite direction:</strong> we examine those machine instructions and reconstruct readable source code that explains how the original program worked.</p>
          <p>That means identifying functions, data structures, control flow, game systems, and the small details that made the shipped program behave the way it did.</p>
        </div>
        <img class="section-icon" src={`${base}images/icons/anvil.webp`} alt="" width="180" height="180" />
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="content-panel" aria-labelledby="matching-title">
      <h2 id="matching-title" class="section-ribbon ribbon-blush">What does “matching” mean?</h2>
      <div class="section-copy">
        <p>A matching decompilation has a useful test: compile the recovered C and compare the result with the original game.</p>

        <div class="matching-cycle" aria-label="Matching decompilation process">
          <div class="cycle-box"><strong>Original game</strong><span>machine code</span></div>
          <b aria-hidden="true">→</b>
          <div class="cycle-box"><strong>Recovered C</strong><span>readable source</span></div>
          <b aria-hidden="true">→</b>
          <div class="cycle-box"><strong>Compile</strong><span>historical toolchain</span></div>
          <b aria-hidden="true">→</b>
          <div class="cycle-box"><strong>Compare</strong><span>instruction by instruction</span></div>
          <b aria-hidden="true">→</b>
          <div class="cycle-box cycle-success"><strong>Match!</strong><span>same machine code</span></div>
        </div>

        <p>If the compiler produces the same instructions, we know the recovered source is not just plausible—it matches the original program at the machine-code level. This gives the project an objective way to measure progress.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="content-panel" aria-labelledby="why-title">
      <h2 id="why-title" class="section-ribbon ribbon-lavender">Why are we doing it?</h2>
      <div class="section-copy illustrated-copy reverse">
        <img class="section-icon" src={`${base}images/icons/progress.webp`} alt="" width="180" height="180" />
        <div>
          <p>Old games contain a great deal of technical knowledge that is otherwise locked inside their binaries. Reconstructing the source makes that implementation understandable again.</p>
          <p>The project preserves knowledge about how the game was built, gives programmers and researchers a way to study a real 1990s PlayStation title, and creates a durable technical record that can outlast the original development environment.</p>
          <p>If you want the detailed numbers, use <a href="https://decomp.dev/celophi/lom-decomp">Progress</a>. If you want the source and build instructions, visit <a href="https://github.com/celophi/lom-decomp">GitHub</a>. Project discoveries and updates will live on the <a href={`${base}blog/`}>blog</a>.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</SiteShell>
